Here's a new Java code that solves the problem of finding the length of the shortest subarray where all elements are different.

### Problem Description
Given an integer array `nums`, find the length of the shortest subarray where all elements are different.

### Code Solution

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Solution {

    public int[] shortestSubarray(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length == 0 || nums.length == 1) return new int[0];

        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i : nums) {
            map.put(i, i);
        }

        int[] minLen = new int[nums.length];
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[nums.length][nums.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < nums.length; j++) {
                if (!map.containsKey(nums[j])) {
                    dp[i][j] = true;
                    minLen[j] = Math.min(minLen[j], j - i + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        int minLen = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (minLen[i] < minLen) {
                minLen = minLen[i];
            }
        }

        Map<Integer, Boolean> uniqueMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
            if (!uniqueMap.containsKey(nums[i])) {
                uniqueMap.put(nums[i], true);
                dp[0][i + 1] = false;
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[nums.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
            if (!uniqueMap.containsKey(nums[i]) || dp[0][i + 1]) {
                result[i] = minLen[i];
            } else {
                result[i] = i + 1;
            }
        }

        return Arrays.copyOf(result, Math.min(minLen, nums.length));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        int[] nums = {1,2,3,4};
        int[] res = solution.shortestSubarray(nums);
        for (int i : res)
            System.out.print(i + " ");
    }
}
```

This code works by maintaining a map `map` that stores the index of each unique element in `nums`. It then uses two nested loops to find all possible subarrays and updates the minimum length.

### Explanation
1. The code first checks if `nums` is empty or has only one element, in which case it returns an array of zeros.
2. Then, it creates a map `map` that stores the index of each unique element in `nums`.
3. It initializes an array `minLen` to store the length of the shortest subarray with all elements different and a boolean array `dp` to keep track of whether each possible subarray is valid.
4. The code then iterates over all possible subarrays using two nested loops, updating `minLen` and `dp`.
5. Once the minimum length is found, it uses another map `uniqueMap` to store the uniqueness of elements in `nums`. It marks the non-unique indices as invalid by setting `dp[0][i + 1]` to false.
6. Finally, it returns an array containing the shortest subarray with all unique elements.

### Time Complexity
O(n^2) where n is the length of `nums`.

### Space Complexity
O(n^2), where n is the number of unique elements in `nums`.
